message.content:
This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. **Primary Request and Intent:**

   Sequential user requests in this session, all in Vietnamese, all concerning the ShieldCanAssy machine's vision calibration:

   - **(1)** Rewrite the guide artifact per the newest workflow, **redrawing block diagrams so a newcomer can understand** ("Bạn hãy cập nhật cả cẩm nang theo WorkFlow mới nhất nhé, nhớ vẽ lại sơ đồ khối để người mới có thể hiểu được").
   - **(2)** Answer how to calibrate the bottom-up camera given it can only see X-axis translation and rotation.
   - **(3)** Re-verify the method choice against page images of the Hikrobot "Calibration and Positioning" PDF the user created at `E:\OneDrive\Tuan\Tuan\HIK\Positioning`.
   - **(4)** Evaluate the top/bottom camera **mapping** method ("dùng một vật mẫu") seen on PDF p.52.
   - **(5)** Find the SDK way to **Set Input of the calib module then Trigger**, to send the gripper's current coordinates with each N-point calibration capture.
   - **(6)** Use **GlobalVariable** instead: set global variables for X, Y1, Y2, A1, A2 before triggering the calib Flow.
   - **(7)** Rewrite the guide per the recommended approach, matching the machine's current concept.
   - **(8)** Produce the **verification report artifact** ("artifact báo cáo kiểm chứng -> tôi cần báo cáo kiểm chứng").

   No security-relevant constraints or restricted-file instructions were stated by the user.

2. **Key Technical Concepts:**

   - **Machine geometry:** CAM top-down rides the gantry (X); the assembly tray moves on Y2; CAM bottom-up is fixed looking up; the product is carried by the gantry and rotated by A1/A2. This creates a **mixed-motion case** for the top camera: moving X moves the *camera* (image moves opposite), moving Y2 moves the *mark on the tray* (image moves with it). A single Camera Mode flag negates **both** axes (PDF p.19: switching mode ≡ reversing the 9-point order), so no value of it fixes a one-axis mismatch.
   - **VisionMaster modules:** N-Point Calibration, Translation Calibration (`CalibTypeEnum.TransCalib` / `TransAndRotateCalib`, `CameraModeEnum.CameraStatic`/`CameraMove`, `HomoFreedomEnum.Perspective/Affine/Similarity`, `CalibPointTotalNum` range **[4,16]**, `RotPointTotalNum` **[0,16]**, `DoClearPoint`, `DoSaveFile`), Rotation Calibration, CalibBoard Calibration, N-image Calibration, Mapping Calibration, Calibration Transformation (`TransPointX/Y/A`), Single Point Alignment (`DeltaX/DeltaY/DeltaTheta`, CW positive), Single Point Map Alignment (needs **both** N Calibration File and MapCalibration File), High Precision Match (`MatchScore` 0…1, Angle Range default −45…45), Variable Calculation (Int→float).
   - **Decisive PDF facts:** p.17 — translation-only calibration yields physical point **B (rotation center)** not point A; rotation calibration "really solves the one-to-one conversion". p.18 §1 — "the 12-point calibration sequence agreed to go S-shape, **but it is not necessary** … if it is calibrated according to the communication protocol, **any trajectory of the machine is possible**". p.18 §3 — calibration **with rotation** puts the fitted rotation-center pixel at **(0,0)**. p.22/p.48 — `TranslationCalibModu` with `Calibration Points Input = By Coordinate`, `Physical Coordinate X ← Protocol Parsing`. p.39 §1 — total deviation = rotation matrix × (teach mark → rotation center) + (running mark → reference mark). p.52 — mapping scenario needs *two* calibrations: mapping **and** "bottom camera translation and rotation calibration".
